The device tree is at a physical address, but the loader hands over a slid one

The loader fills boot_args.deviceTreeP with the kernel's virtual base plus
the tree's offset from the kernel's own load address, so the number in it
is a virtual address in the kernel's space.

Nothing can read that number here.  This is the first pass over the
platform, before arm_vm_init() has built a single mapping of the kernel's
virtual space, and the early page table the payload set up maps physical
addresses one to one, with nothing above 0x200000000 mapped at all.  The
kernel's virtual base is not in that table, so the address is not merely
unreadable, it is not there.

That is why the tree never arrived.  SecureDTInit() was handed an address
that holds nothing, and InitIOKit() -- which makes the root nub and gives
it its name from the tree -- was handed the same address, so the root nub
came up with no name and nothing that matches on "device-tree" was ever
offered it.  The platform expert that reads the tree never started, the
nodes under /arm-io were never published, and the controller was never
matched.  No amount of fixing the tree itself could have helped: the
kernel was not looking at it.

What SecureDTInit() and InitIOKit() need is the tree itself, and the tree
is at a physical address, which the early page table can read.  The two
bases in the boot arguments say how far the virtual address is from it.

Take the slide off and the tree is found.  arm_vm_init() puts the slide
back on later, in PE_slide_devicetree(), once the mapping exists; the two
together leave the tree where it was and let it be read in between.

---

diff --git a/pexpert/arm/pe_init.c b/pexpert/arm/pe_init.c
--- a/pexpert/arm/pe_init.c
+++ b/pexpert/arm/pe_init.c
@@ -409,6 +409,45 @@
 		PE_state.bootArgs = boot_args_ptr;
 		PE_state.deviceTreeHead = boot_args_ptr->deviceTreeP;
 		PE_state.deviceTreeSize = boot_args_ptr->deviceTreeLength;
